the following sets are closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 1}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2x-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2b183-1fbb-5d3a-95b8-ba5907b77381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449964} {:answer/id #uuid "009859f9-d5f0-5f12-8808-874a29b0d49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009859f9-d5f0-5f12-8808-874a29b0d49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449965} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e233f5-f964-5ef9-8385-488a93458b37" :answer-field/key "$\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a4adba-909c-5b52-9d39-dd7435fea047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449966} {:answer/id #uuid "8350d841-99c9-5e4f-bef6-5096ca7e6bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8350d841-99c9-5e4f-bef6-5096ca7e6bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449967} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b347b8-8595-51cb-990e-7a0cbef944cf" :answer-field/key "$A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c563f6e6-481a-566e-952a-6ebc338f042e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449968} {:answer/id #uuid "2b815ef2-94ab-5ef5-96a3-fca6c27ac9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c563f6e6-481a-566e-952a-6ebc338f042e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449968} :answer-field/id #uuid "4645f2ba-58bb-53a2-860c-75230a1dd41f" :answer-field/key "$∂A⊂A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement $∂A⊂A$ is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$; $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bb52348-a50b-5509-94d7-e312e15e922b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465441} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d1d18c-2c18-5ba9-a875-75dec189ca03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d1d18c-2c18-5ba9-a875-75dec189ca03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465442} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a0fc8-fc1c-5e05-86df-321963effe9c" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<|2x|\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e179f35-3e64-5a2d-bdbd-cec179c87f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465443} {:answer/id #uuid "45531787-af9f-5f77-a4c4-b03440b8640e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e179f35-3e64-5a2d-bdbd-cec179c87f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465443} :answer-field/id #uuid "89701a6c-6d0c-5c54-be79-8e42f1681ba3" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd1affc-9370-5696-bd27-febdf87917ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465445} {:answer/id #uuid "bce5f56d-f9db-58ab-8ded-169f93431baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce5f56d-f9db-58ab-8ded-169f93431baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465446} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1bdf075-be73-58e7-822c-d788c0385547" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3f47e10-181c-587e-a0be-2b1e263782c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161646" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<|2x|\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bed96ed-825c-5193-bb36-6d9f347edbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465447} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a2580-c02f-5398-8a94-8ca13da500c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bed96ed-825c-5193-bb36-6d9f347edbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465447} :answer-field/id #uuid "a93e410a-2dc7-5269-9067-7517a0f7a2ac" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6cbe163-fc27-5d7a-86ba-be062bc203bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465449} {:answer/id #uuid "e724a00c-d190-5dc0-a9a6-df5aab6d3ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e724a00c-d190-5dc0-a9a6-df5aab6d3ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465450} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4281105-6e70-5eec-9b40-407806b14a8e" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\\\\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149425c6-0498-5c99-a352-0ec427a109a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465451} {:answer/id #uuid "23d20095-cdfb-5bd4-99da-2d9b72a8cfda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d20095-cdfb-5bd4-99da-2d9b72a8cfda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465452} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ec6c3d-c76a-58bb-b31c-ebf8bf37fb63" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdca40a5-5172-5279-8887-174acedd2d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-161647" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\\\\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3140a798-db1e-5f78-87be-0ad6910ccf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449970} {:answer/id #uuid "66652cf1-121a-55d6-b60b-7370a097e440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3140a798-db1e-5f78-87be-0ad6910ccf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449970} :answer-field/id #uuid "0239bed3-bff6-57a4-914e-a2a7696a453b" :answer-field/key "$A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51696bef-50a4-5444-9a43-36db6f9afa18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449972} {:answer/id #uuid "42fe6b05-f33a-59cb-bdd4-1fe79fe94856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fe6b05-f33a-59cb-bdd4-1fe79fe94856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449973} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fcaecef-8c7d-5939-af0c-a44de76b6473" :answer-field/key "$A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25836713-0be8-5121-b3d9-e6b2691cc476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449974} {:answer/id #uuid "d419b241-208a-5a0c-b5ad-26a22859c22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25836713-0be8-5121-b3d9-e6b2691cc476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449974} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7ad3f5-382d-59a9-a467-493359e62446" :answer-field/key "$∂A⊂A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91207617-bfa9-5efa-a562-b7d640b0c541" :question/math-academy-id "q-161651" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$ and $∂A⊂A$ are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa99ca1-0112-5944-8107-a465cf0627dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449976} {:answer/id #uuid "15a110f6-eccf-56f5-998c-8997e94f0e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fa99ca1-0112-5944-8107-a465cf0627dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449976} :answer-field/id #uuid "064a7a52-cec6-5ccc-a004-9c6fecd644e0" :answer-field/key "$∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327300e8-f0e1-5c3f-a6e7-beb22904b216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449978} {:answer/id #uuid "50af4e2e-bd80-5f87-9f7f-30c9a6d776f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50af4e2e-bd80-5f87-9f7f-30c9a6d776f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449979} :answer-field/id #uuid "72039e1e-3581-51dc-a5b8-57794c0a1d42" :answer-field/key "$\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450943} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d1b1a8-bff4-538e-9246-7df95c62a467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449980} {:answer/id #uuid "22eb3a74-db0a-5456-85b9-2111511cf1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22eb3a74-db0a-5456-85b9-2111511cf1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449981} :answer-field/id #uuid "1116eaca-e1e8-5e9d-879d-508f9c37e751" :answer-field/key "$A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$ is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$; $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddcb03ab-ffc7-5dde-a933-7374df237b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449982} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ecb14-3118-537c-b265-36ac3c5c4e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ecb14-3118-537c-b265-36ac3c5c4e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449983} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db9f7f0-2fbe-5bfb-88f5-650ddb13358c" :answer-field/key "The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bbec439-5029-5663-98a6-7e32d79eaa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449984} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e4b64f-bd71-5313-87a7-f6c616739ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e4b64f-bd71-5313-87a7-f6c616739ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449985} :answer-field/id #uuid "300d5e5c-ad59-5480-a520-0c1afdf364a1" :answer-field/key "The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec3ff93-228e-542f-8812-a0b344b17573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449986} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c77c6-5431-5dbf-9223-a1c8fe1ec4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c77c6-5431-5dbf-9223-a1c8fe1ec4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449987} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c4ed37-cec0-5927-8a59-1ff0216677f6" :answer-field/key "The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9d32a6-dbbc-54ba-9b9d-46f810008dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$” is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement “The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$” is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$” is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$”; “The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$”; “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f3745d-f09c-5360-a931-21a667dd1d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465453} {:answer/id #uuid "21ed980e-4000-5687-a17b-d84e6f1e4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ed980e-4000-5687-a17b-d84e6f1e4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8bbd34-591c-5d0f-b655-42fc58838887" :answer-field/key "$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835fd9d2-ac5f-5fc7-a341-f53a230e3899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} {:answer/id #uuid "1205f76c-8469-5ede-a981-4f167eb37530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "835fd9d2-ac5f-5fc7-a341-f53a230e3899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92c5843-42f4-52a3-a501-b15764eb3a62" :answer-field/key "${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cd71c2-430d-5fd7-a358-b102308c8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} {:answer/id #uuid "71481a29-9e86-5894-95fe-7fecf6d21f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36cd71c2-430d-5fd7-a358-b102308c8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465457} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98b628c-ca32-5dd8-8891-b556d9bd4149" :answer-field/key "The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$” is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$” is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$” is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1257faba-9e7b-5cca-8ae5-84b10e9a9aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465459} {:answer/id #uuid "38ddbd87-a974-5584-bfba-bd3e8c19432d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1257faba-9e7b-5cca-8ae5-84b10e9a9aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465459} :answer-field/id #uuid "41153c5d-1a60-56f9-9889-e3734c88b5fc" :answer-field/key "$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57884e71-d5eb-506d-a128-dafffc6cb068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465461} {:answer/id #uuid "20be9fdc-8871-57ef-a9b8-8c9a211d7e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57884e71-d5eb-506d-a128-dafffc6cb068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465461} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e0fc32-e0b5-5737-beea-a8d93563b24b" :answer-field/key "${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d127b5a-2c09-5d77-b9cd-0489463d1715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465463} {:answer/id #uuid "9deb9bc4-9347-59f4-82b8-fc109454910a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9deb9bc4-9347-59f4-82b8-fc109454910a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465464} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ddba5e6-8fb0-518c-b7d8-7cac2d5ca0ba" :answer-field/key "The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "649157e5-32cb-5583-98fc-af0b21c39379" :question/math-academy-id "q-161858" :question/problem "![](images/54/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$” is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement “${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$” is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$” is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6482623d-8c4e-5651-a72b-e7411d79d809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465465} {:answer/id #uuid "939961c0-1305-54a9-818b-09cfc0d38502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939961c0-1305-54a9-818b-09cfc0d38502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465466} :answer-field/id #uuid "82301762-7b0f-595d-b514-4ced579d9cbf" :answer-field/key "$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466576} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6687532f-6a13-5099-9722-b86b313bd075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465467} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ff3f4-5cfd-5289-a27a-38a8b986e0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6687532f-6a13-5099-9722-b86b313bd075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465467} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b945cf-8f03-5126-b845-2288e60189d1" :answer-field/key "${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8985717d-57ba-54c8-9bc4-d11aee23fe95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465469} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a7dd18-8d8e-5642-834e-a057e86e24aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8985717d-57ba-54c8-9bc4-d11aee23fe95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465469} :answer-field/id #uuid "e731320a-3d91-5595-9f7e-2db38ae1357f" :answer-field/key "The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c687527-ddc3-50e7-9a3f-531649897edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161865" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$” is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0.5,1.5),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$” is false. Notice that $y=-0.88=f(0),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$” is also false. Notice that $y=2.5=f(3).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=3$ since ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2c270a8-c691-513a-a6e6-0d9bc6a07b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :d